WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.335 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:03.335 --> 00:00:05.504
Well, it's been two weeks,
and we're just going

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:06.940
to hit the ground running.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:12.578
Right before the break, I played
a southern lawyer pretty well,

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:16.583
and we showed a picture
of a lawyer in a bolo tie.

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:19.686
And many of you reached out
and said a bolo tie

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:23.055
would be more accurate for
a western

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:25.024
or a southwestern lawyer.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:27.259
A bow tie would have been
more appropriate

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:29.395
for a southern lawyer.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.831
And I do believe it was
southern lawyers who

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.700
were upset about this because
most of the comments began,

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:38.537
"I take umbrage!"

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:43.376
The amount...of umbrage.

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:47.613
Oh, so, obviously, we all know
that Mike Lindell

00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:50.783
got his cell phone seized
at Hardy's.

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.085
And so in yesterday's
Closer Look,

00:00:53.085 --> 00:00:55.454
we basically said, "What's next?
Is he going to get

00:00:55.454 --> 00:00:58.824
his camera seized
at Carl's Jr.?"

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:02.295
Hardee's and Carl's Jr.,
they have the same ownership.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:03.797
Minnesota only has a Hardee's.

00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:06.031
There wouldn't be a Carl's Jr.
there.

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:09.168
And the bummer about this is...

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:12.004
And it bothers me.
And you saw it. You heard it,

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:15.474
Shoemaker.
We -- I have a producer

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:16.642
on A Closer Look, Emily.

00:01:16.642 --> 00:01:18.344
She has a great attention
to detail.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.613
And I heard her tell Sal
we're going to hear

00:01:20.613 --> 00:01:24.417
from the jackals about this
if you put it in.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.687
But, Sal, he just couldn't
care less.

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:30.256
I mean, he couldn't care less
because Sal, who does --

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:32.024
by the way, does a great job
with A Closer Look.

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:33.292
I have no complaints
about his effort,

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:37.129
but when he turns it in,
he checks out.

00:01:37.129 --> 00:01:41.467
I mean, he slaps it --
he just slaps it on the desk,

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.736
walks out of the building,
gets in his car service,

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:47.006
goes right to the marina,
gets in his boat.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.008
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:52.745
The Hotel Salifornia,
he takes it down.

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:55.849
Takes it downtown, meets
all his Wall Street buddies

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:58.283
as they're getting -- like, they
walk out of the trading floor

00:01:58.283 --> 00:02:00.987
and Sal's just waiting there,
like Wolf of Wall Street.

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:02.988
Leo DiCaprio.

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:07.526
Big, big --
He's got a wrap-around steps

00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:08.561
on his, like, boat.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:11.698
And he's like, "Hey, gentlemen."

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:14.300
And they're like, "Sally, did
you fight the plutocracy today?"

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:20.173
And he's like, "[Laughs] They're
paying my bills, fellas."

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:23.176
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:29.148
We showed this

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:33.920
and we referred to it
as Jigsaw from the movie "Saw."

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.924
This is Billy the Puppet
from the movie "Saw,"

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:41.594
not the killer, Jigsaw.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.463
This is a photo of Jigsaw.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:46.632
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:49.134
Also this, I referred to this
as a quarter moon.

00:02:49.134 --> 00:02:53.339
It's a waning -- you can
call it a waning or a crescent.

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:56.376
Sorry, a waning
or a waxing crescent moon.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:58.610
You can, of course, also call it

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.846
the last thing you see

00:03:00.846 --> 00:03:03.149
as the life
is being choked out of you.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.018
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:11.457
I did an impression
of a 19th-century dandy

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:14.160
choosing a Charleston Chew
as his treat.

00:03:16.395 --> 00:03:18.231
And they said you couldn't --

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:20.066
that wouldn't happen because
the Charleston Chew

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:22.735
wasn't invented
until the 20th century, in 1925.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:25.038
But bear with me.
What if you were

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:28.240
a 19th century dandy in England,

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.075
and as a child, you know,
you would get excited

00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:33.078
about candy, sweets.

00:03:33.078 --> 00:03:35.381
And then, you know, life takes
you in a different path.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.983
You take a steamship to America,
try to make your way there.

00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:42.488
You're older now, but still,
you're in your youth.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:44.823
You were a dandy.
And now it's 1925,

00:03:44.823 --> 00:03:49.561
and there's been a breakthrough
in the confectionery...

00:03:49.561 --> 00:03:53.899
industries, and you're presented
with a Charleston Chew.

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:58.403
Isn't it possible, like the film
"Ratatouille," as you oft

00:03:58.403 --> 00:04:01.707
reference, Shoemaker.

00:04:01.707 --> 00:04:04.610
That that moment of seeing it
would bring you back

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:05.711
to your 19th century?

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:09.015
You'd be like,
"Whoo-hoo! [Laughs]"

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:10.116
[ Laughter ]

00:04:10.116 --> 00:04:11.784
Use your imagination --
sometimes --

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:14.152
We can a lot of the work here,
but we'd love

00:04:14.152 --> 00:04:16.055
if you could use your
imagination for a little bit.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:19.459
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:23.563
I mimed someone doing --
giving --

00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:25.832
doing CPR
and giving mouth to mouth.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:29.869
I was told you don't do mouth
to mouth during CPR anymore,

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.270
just compressions.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.540
So thanks, cancel culture.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.577
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:47.153
That brings me to this point.

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.355
Someone wrote they did not
care for Corrections.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:51.657
[ Laughter ]

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:53.292
They had multiple comments
about it, which meant

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:57.596
they didn't care for it
and stuck it out.

00:04:57.596 --> 00:05:00.866
At one point, they wrote,
"There are five people

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:03.803
in the audience. Wow.
All that wokeness is paying off,

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.071
huh, Seth?"

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:07.740
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.743
Look, you can feel how you want
to feel about Corrections,

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:13.779
but the idea that we want it
to be full

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:17.784
and can only get five people
because it's too woke?

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:20.053
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.621
Uh, yeah.

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:24.957
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:28.327
And we showed
this photo of a gopher.

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:30.562
And then a couple of minutes
later we said --

00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:33.866
we basically said a fellow was
trying to catch a gopher,

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:36.235
and then the gopher stole
his beer,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.170
and we showed a photo of this.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.607
And someone said. "Hey,
not to be the person

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:44.944
who knows a bunch about animals,
but that might very well

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:47.446
be a gopher, but that's
definitely a woodchuck."

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:50.883
So here's what happened. Okay?

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:52.751
You know, we write A Closer Look
and then, you know,

00:05:52.751 --> 00:05:54.353
obviously, these jokes go in,

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:56.089
and then we reach out
to our talent booker

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.991
and we're like,
"We need a gopher."

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:02.194
And so we bring in this gopher,
and he comes in, crushes it.

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:05.230
He was great. Did exactly
what we needed him to do.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:06.365
And then we're like,

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:07.867
"Alright, now you got
to hold the beer."

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:11.370
And he was like,
"Oh.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.840
Like, I'm a Mormon."
And we were like, "What?"

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.542
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.611
And we were like,
"It's not even full of beer."

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:21.313
And he's like, "Yeah, but I got
kids and, you know..."

00:06:21.313 --> 00:06:23.649
We were like, "Oh, no.
Obviously, we respect that."

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:26.919
And so, we were like,
"We're going to obviously

00:06:26.919 --> 00:06:28.387
have to bring somebody else in."

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:30.255
And he's like, "[ Sighs ]

00:06:30.255 --> 00:06:31.791
Are you going to have to
use them for the first picture?

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:33.526
Because I told a lot of people
I was going to be on tonight."

00:06:33.526 --> 00:06:35.795
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.795 --> 00:06:37.263
And we're like,
"No, we can still use you

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:39.398
for the first picture because
it's not like some psychopath

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:43.202
is going to be watching
and be like, 'Uh.'"

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:46.272
This guy, on the other hand,
came in and kept drinking them.

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.607
[ Laughter ]

00:06:48.607 --> 00:06:50.977
It was not a good scene.

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:52.444
[ Laughter ]

00:06:52.444 --> 00:06:55.947
When John and Jim were
hauling him out.

00:06:55.947 --> 00:06:57.717
"Look out for his teeth!"

00:06:57.717 --> 00:07:00.753
[ Laughter ]

00:07:02.154 --> 00:07:05.158
[ Laughs ]

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:11.297
The whole rest of the week,
every time...

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:14.333
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.172 --> 00:07:23.108
Every time I walked into
the office,

00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:26.045
I can't do it.
[ Laughs ]

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:29.081
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:35.187
Someone said, "I missed when
Amber was there

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:37.355
during Corrections because
I loved hearing her laugh."

00:07:37.355 --> 00:07:39.191
Oh, she's still here.

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:42.228
[ Laughter ]

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:46.832
Someone wrote,
"I'm a foreign viewer,

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:48.700
and this whole time, I thought
the Mets and the Jets

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:50.303
were the same thing."

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:53.205
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:56.709
They're very different,
but the usage is similar.

00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:59.311
Like, if you're ever visiting
New York and you say

00:07:59.311 --> 00:08:00.645
"Mets, amiright?"

00:08:00.645 --> 00:08:02.481
Works the same with Jets.

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:04.450
[ Laughter ]

00:08:06.285 --> 00:08:10.456
Oh, so during last week's
Correction, at one point

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:14.327
I said, "Hey, can you..."
Oh.

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:18.097
There was a sound effect,
and I wanted there to be

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:20.933
lightning and then
the thunder --

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:22.634
the thunder sound effect,
excuse me.

00:08:22.634 --> 00:08:25.838
And then after it played,
I said, "They wouldn't play

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:27.840
the lightning sound effect."

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.009
And a lot of you guys said,
"Well, there wouldn't be --

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:31.410
You mean thunder sound effect?

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.079
There's no lightning
sound effect."

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:37.749
And what happened was I did
want a thunder sound effect,

00:08:37.749 --> 00:08:39.084
but I accidentally
said lightning.

00:08:39.084 --> 00:08:41.253
Not then, earlier in the day,
I said to Alex,

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:43.121
"Hey, will you play me
a lightning sound effect?"

00:08:43.121 --> 00:08:44.990
And he said, "Sure thing."

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:47.593
And then it happened
and there was no sound effect.

00:08:47.593 --> 00:08:49.194
And then I went to him and said,
"Why no sound effect?"

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:52.130
And he said, "You asked for
a lightning sound effect.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:53.599
And there isn't one."

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:55.934
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:59.005
And I was like, "So you thought
I wanted nothing?"

00:08:59.005 --> 00:09:01.506
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.506 --> 00:09:03.142
And he just stared at me.

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:06.679
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:10.149
They yell, "Go easy on him!"
[ Laughs ]

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:13.185
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:25.997
We talked about when you see
like ye old curiosity shoppe,

00:09:25.997 --> 00:09:30.236
you shouldn't say "ye,"
you should say "the" old,

00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:33.605
because there was a letter back
in the day called a thorn,

00:09:33.605 --> 00:09:35.374
and it was close to a "Y,"

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:38.443
and the thorn
was the sound "Th."

00:09:38.443 --> 00:09:43.115
When you say "Y-E" in "ye," it's
actually "T-H-E, the."

00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:48.887
and many of you pointed out that
if "Y" is pronounced "Th,"

00:09:48.887 --> 00:09:54.192
that means that Kanye's new name
isn't Ye, but The.

00:09:54.192 --> 00:09:57.496
Which is, of course,
short for The Antisemitic.

00:09:57.496 --> 00:10:00.966
[ Laughter ]

00:10:00.966 --> 00:10:03.569
[Autotuned] ♪ Why you got to be
so heartless? ♪

00:10:03.569 --> 00:10:06.606
[ Laughter ]

00:10:15.381 --> 00:10:17.516
I'm taken aback
at how well that worked.

00:10:17.516 --> 00:10:20.752
[ Laughter ]

00:10:20.752 --> 00:10:24.090
One week, you're getting
no thunder sound effect.

00:10:28.928 --> 00:10:33.766
Mike Nesmith's mom,
from The Monkeys, did not --

00:10:33.766 --> 00:10:36.735
I said she invented WiteOut.

00:10:36.735 --> 00:10:41.040
She invented Liquid Paper.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.643
WiteOut was invented by
a woman named Barbara Dolans.

00:10:44.643 --> 00:10:48.013
[ Laughter ]

00:10:48.013 --> 00:10:52.585
[Autotuned] ♪ Cheer up,
sleepy -- ♪ [ Laughs ]

00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:55.855
[ Laughter ]

00:11:00.992 --> 00:11:04.296
Oh, my God. Oh.

00:11:04.296 --> 00:11:06.999
Well. [ Whoops ] Hold on.

00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:10.536
So this is pretty cool.

00:11:10.536 --> 00:11:12.571
Uh...

00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:16.708
Someone sent in --
alright, hold on.

00:11:16.708 --> 00:11:17.977
How I am going to do this
and not give it away?

00:11:17.977 --> 00:11:24.216
Alright, so, someone sent to our
new P.O. Box a note

00:11:24.216 --> 00:11:26.084
that said, "For a while,
it seemed like Wally

00:11:26.084 --> 00:11:28.054
Was getting all the fan art."
Which was true.

00:11:28.054 --> 00:11:29.922
We've shown a fair amount
of Wally fan art.

00:11:29.922 --> 00:11:32.758
"I didn't want there
to be a weird monopoly

00:11:32.758 --> 00:11:34.492
where there was this gold
sitting right there."

00:11:34.492 --> 00:11:36.461
With this whole run up,
I thought it was going to be

00:11:36.461 --> 00:11:39.698
fan art -- me fan art.
Right?

00:11:39.698 --> 00:11:41.300
Because there's been too much,
Wally, and you know,

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:43.569
it is my show.
And, but it was --

00:11:43.569 --> 00:11:45.437
Look at the Scollins fan art.

00:11:45.437 --> 00:11:48.474
[ Laughter ]

00:11:53.212 --> 00:11:56.015
T.J.. Thank you, T.J.,
for doing this.

00:11:56.015 --> 00:11:58.950
He also said, "Also, I mocked up
an actual sleeveless shirt

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:00.519
if the merch store runs out
of Jackal mugs."

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:03.956
So I'm wondering
if this is -- you would get

00:12:03.956 --> 00:12:07.926
this printed on a tank top.

00:12:07.926 --> 00:12:09.661
I think that would be
pretty cool.

00:12:09.661 --> 00:12:12.264
But then I should know there's
a --

00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:15.200
This is wonderful,
and I hope Pim,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.103
I think, is the name
of the artist for this,

00:12:17.103 --> 00:12:22.441
from Thailand
sent this painting of me.

00:12:22.441 --> 00:12:24.009
How great is that?

00:12:24.009 --> 00:12:26.545
And I'm really --
I'm really touched by this.

00:12:26.545 --> 00:12:30.682
And it got a very --
a very lovely compliment.

00:12:30.682 --> 00:12:33.252
I brought this home
and I showed my wife

00:12:33.252 --> 00:12:35.053
and she said the sweetest thing.

00:12:35.053 --> 00:12:37.790
She said, "You should keep
that at the office."

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:40.826
[ Laughter ]

00:12:42.995 --> 00:12:47.165
Guess she sees enough of my mug.
[ Laughs ]

00:12:47.165 --> 00:12:50.603
And then you guys, I mean,
we have talked about this,

00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:53.372
but we have our P.O. Box
and we --

00:12:53.372 --> 00:12:55.307
it's just there's a lot of --

00:12:55.307 --> 00:12:57.543
we are getting
a lot of postcards.

00:12:57.543 --> 00:13:00.179
And as I told you,
that is a legal problem

00:13:00.179 --> 00:13:02.047
because this is --

00:13:02.047 --> 00:13:04.416
look at this,
this stack alone,

00:13:04.416 --> 00:13:05.784
that's just Europe.

00:13:05.784 --> 00:13:07.352
I mean, it's amazing,
what we're getting.

00:13:07.352 --> 00:13:09.221
This is so --
Look at that.

00:13:09.221 --> 00:13:11.223
You know,
that's Washington Square Park.

00:13:11.223 --> 00:13:16.228
[ Laughter ]

00:13:16.228 --> 00:13:19.298
The Loch Ness monster.
That's so exciting. Malta?!

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:21.766
Are you kidding me, Malta?
They're coming in from all over.

00:13:21.766 --> 00:13:23.402
But like I said,
there's a problem,

00:13:23.402 --> 00:13:25.404
because I guess
I should not have said

00:13:25.404 --> 00:13:28.139
we're going to do a postcard
drawing because of that.

00:13:28.139 --> 00:13:30.209
Due to the legal rules --

00:13:30.209 --> 00:13:32.277
According to legal department,
that is a problematic thing,

00:13:32.277 --> 00:13:35.013
and we might be in the jackpot,
so to speak.

00:13:35.013 --> 00:13:38.283
But then I talked to the legal
team, who are not kind people,

00:13:38.283 --> 00:13:42.687
and also I try not to call them
because every time I do,

00:13:42.687 --> 00:13:45.524
they bill me.
But I said, "Well, wait,

00:13:45.524 --> 00:13:50.562
hold on. I have an idea.
Because, you know, the thing is,

00:13:50.562 --> 00:13:53.899
all I said was, like, send them
in and then I'm going to do

00:13:53.899 --> 00:13:55.634
a postcard drawing."

00:13:55.634 --> 00:13:57.936
And then I realized maybe there
is a way out of this.

00:13:57.936 --> 00:14:00.872
Maybe legally,
I could just do --

00:14:00.872 --> 00:14:02.308
I could just do this.

00:14:02.308 --> 00:14:06.945
I could just like -- what if I
did a drawing of a postcard?

00:14:06.945 --> 00:14:10.415
[ Laughter ]

00:14:10.415 --> 00:14:13.118
We'll see.
But again, you have to --

00:14:13.118 --> 00:14:14.686
Here's the thing
that I really want to stress.

00:14:14.686 --> 00:14:17.122
You got
to stop sending postcards

00:14:17.122 --> 00:14:21.326
because it's a problem for me.
It's legal exposure.

00:14:21.326 --> 00:14:24.396
So just forget You ever heard
P.O. Box 4748,

00:14:24.396 --> 00:14:28.033
New York, New York, 10185.

00:14:28.033 --> 00:14:31.870
Because it has not been
good for me.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:37.776
[ Laughter ]

00:14:37.776 --> 00:14:39.377
Go easy on John, though.

00:14:39.377 --> 00:14:40.512
[ Bleep ].

00:14:40.512 --> 00:14:43.315
[ Laughter ]

00:14:43.315 --> 00:14:47.085
You guys, I am really happy
to be back with you,

00:14:47.085 --> 00:14:51.924
and I really do hope
that you all see me next week.

